# Symbols where Firecracker's base config and a vanilla kernel.org tree are
# allowed to disagree. Firecracker publishes those configs for the Amazon Linux
# microVM kernel and does not guarantee them against kernel.org sources, so this
# file is the price of using them off-label: every difference is named once, with
# a reason, and anything not listed here fails the build.
#
# Symbols nibrun.config sets are exempt automatically — overriding is the point.

# Regenerated from the toolchain, never carried from a config.
CONFIG_CC_VERSION_TEXT
CONFIG_GCC_VERSION
CONFIG_AS_VERSION
CONFIG_LD_VERSION
CONFIG_PAHOLE_VERSION

# Amazon Linux-only symbols that vanilla has never had. Both are `is not set`
# upstream, so nothing is lost by their absence.
CONFIG_AMAZON_DRIVER_UPDATES
CONFIG_DMA_PAGE_TOUCHING

# Amazon Linux carries this =y; vanilla 6.1 has no such driver. It is a second
# wall-clock source for snapshot resume. Ours is kvmclock, which nibrun.config
# asserts.
CONFIG_PTP_1588_CLOCK_VMCLOCK

# Amazon Linux out-of-tree driver: /dev/sysgenid is the generation counter
# userspace watches to know a snapshot was restored and reseed a PRNG of its own.
# The kernel's half of that is already covered — CONFIG_VMGENID=y is in the base
# config and asserted by nibrun.config, so Firecracker's generation-id interrupt
# reseeds the CRNG before the vCPUs run and getrandom() comes back fresh. What is
# left is a tenant that seeded its own PRNG and never asks the kernel again, and
# that duplicates nothing unless one snapshot is restored more than once.
# **Restore-once is the invariant that keeps this line cheap; giving it up is what
# would make moving the build onto the Amazon Linux source tree worth it.**
CONFIG_SYSGENID

# Oversampling rate for the jitter entropy RNG; the symbol does not exist in
# vanilla 6.1. Not load-bearing here — CONFIG_RANDOM_TRUST_CPU and virtio-rng
# are what seed the CRNG, and both are asserted.
CONFIG_CRYPTO_JITTERENTROPY_OSR
